// Rendered at container start by docker/entrypoint.sh. One published image serves every
// deployment (§16.1), so nothing about which chain, which wallet origin or which label this
// dApp uses is baked in at build time.
//
// A plain <script> rather than a fetched /config.json, deliberately — and this is the one
// place custom-example departs from what wallet-web does:
//
//   src/giano.ts builds `provider` and `publicClient` at MODULE level from these values, and
//   src/config.ts exports `chain`, `chainB` and `demoChains` as module-level constants. All
//   of them therefore have to be readable SYNCHRONOUSLY by the time the bundle evaluates. A
//   script tag ahead of the module bundle guarantees that for the cost of one line in
//   index.html; fetching would mean making every one of those exports async and threading a
//   loaded-config object through giano.ts, App.tsx and WalletPanel.tsx.
//
// wallet-web can fetch because its App takes the config as a prop and it has no module-level
// clients. The environment contract — the GIANO_* variables — is identical either way, which
// is the part the deployment actually depends on.
window.__GIANO_CONFIG__ = {
  chainId: ${GIANO_CHAIN_ID},
  chainName: '${GIANO_CHAIN_NAME}',
  rpcUrl: '${GIANO_RPC_URL}',
  // 0 disables the second chain and the cross-chain panel with it.
  chainBId: ${GIANO_CHAIN_B_ID},
  chainBName: '${GIANO_CHAIN_B_NAME}',
  rpcBUrl: '${GIANO_RPC_B_URL}',
  // The TENANT's wallet origin, and each dApp is pinned to exactly one of them.
  walletUrl: '${GIANO_WALLET_URL}',
  // Empty strings are treated as unset by src/config.ts.
  appLabel: '${GIANO_APP_LABEL}',
  testErc20: '${GIANO_TEST_ERC20}',
};
